export default "---\nname: \"qbank-debrief\"\ndescription: \"Judge a finished UWorld block in this chat and review it with Mo, saving as he talks, review a UWorld question in Chrome in one hub call, log one question-bank miss (no card) with what AnKing and Mehlman say on the topic, or make the day's flashcards. Use when Mo says \\\"send the block\\\" or \\\"upload the block\\\" after a UWorld block, asks about a UWorld question he has open (\\\"this one, I picked C\\\", \\\"why is it D\\\"), says \\\"log this\\\", \\\"lts\\\", \\\"ltl\\\", \\\"ltg\\\", \\\"make flashcards\\\" or \\\"make a card on X\\\".\"\n---\n\n# Qbank debrief\n\nMo is studying for USMLE Step 2 CK (exam day) and wants a high score. Four jobs:\n\n0. **\"Send the block\"** (or \"upload the block\"), after a UWorld block, when Mo has pressed Stop on the\n   recorder: read the block's pages, judge every question here by the hub's own rules, sum it up, then take him\n   through the curated review in this chat, saving as he talks. See \"Sending a block\" and \"The curated review\"\n   below (Mo, 1 Nov 2026: one instruction, his subscription, the review in the same chat).\n\n1. **Reviewing a UWorld block in Chrome** (Mo, 27 Oct 2026). Once the block is judged (\"send the block\" in this\n   chat, or the hub's own judge when that setting is on), the hub holds a pack for every wrong or shaky question,\n   so each question is **one** Practice Hub call: see \"UWorld review\" below. It must feel instant.\n2. **\"Log this\"** (or \"lts\" = \"I was sure\", \"ltl\" = \"I leaned, down to two\", \"ltg\" = \"I guessed\"): **log the miss only, with no card**, and reply\n   briefly with the rule plus what AnKing and Mehlman say about the topic. On UWorld that is the same one call\n   with `log: true`; elsewhere (NBME, a typed case) see Step 1.\n3. **\"Make flashcards\"** (or \"make me cards\") at the end of the day: **suggest at most 15 cards from the day's misses\n   worth the most marks. Mo keeps as many as he wants, and you add only those** (Mo, 22 Oct and 1 Nov 2026). Never\n   make a card unless Mo asks.\n\nHow you do each step is your call: what follows is what Mo values and what the system needs, not rules to\nfollow mechanically.\n\n## What Mo values\n\n- Cards that test what the exam keeps testing. UWorld's Educational Objective is usually the heart of it.\n  The AnKing cards tagged with the question (call **anking_cards** with the question ID) show what the\n  community deck rates as high yield there; use whatever helps, on the card, in its notes or in your\n  reply. Mehlman Medical's high-yield PDFs are searchable too: call **high_yield_notes** (see \"Searching\n  Mehlman\" below) and fold in what they add, usually in the notes after the Why, on the card itself when\n  it is the point. Neither source has to add anything: a good card without them is fine. If a lookup\n  finds nothing or fails, carry on.\n- Cards that sound right spoken: Mo reviews by voice, so plain words and full sentences, no arrows or\n  symbols. US guidelines.\n- Cases that feel like the real question: built from the question's own deciding clues, concise enough to\n  hear read aloud.\n- Notes worth reading on screen: a memorable \"Why\" first, then anything that genuinely helps (mnemonics,\n  numbers, exceptions, US versus UK differences).\n- Pictures. Mo likes them (23 Oct 2026). Give every card its best pictures, up to three (card_review says\n  the same), one from each source when they show different things (for example an NBME x-ray plus an AnKing comparison table).\n  Sources in Mo's order:\n  1. His **NBME High Yield Images** photo PDF: the pictures high_yield_notes returns whose names start\n     nbme_, used when the caption names this card's finding.\n  2. Mehlman and AnKing. Mehlman's PDFs are text only on the server, so their part is the notes. For\n     AnKing, use his **AnKing Step 2 High Yield picture library** of summary tables and graphics: the\n     high_yield_notes pictures whose names start anking_hy_, plus anything in **picture_catalogue** (the\n     whole library in one call; once per batch is enough) that covers the card's topic. Keep a low bar:\n     add one whenever it covers the topic, even broadly, for example \"Upper versus lower motor neuron\n     lesions\" on a pronator drift card. Then use the pictures on the AnKing cards for the question\n     (anking_cards), then other AnKing cards on the topic (find_cards with all_decks).\n  3. A Wikimedia Commons file page, only when nothing above shows the finding.\n  Put library pictures on with images {library: <name>}. Look at an AnKing card picture (get_card_images)\n  before trusting its file name; never add or show one that does not fit. In Claude Code, send it with SendUserFile\n  (display render), with caption and credit. By voice, describe it in one sentence.\n- Speed. The exam pace is 90 seconds a question; over 2 minutes is slow. A topic he is slow on is weak even\n  when he got it right, so every log keeps the time (Mo, 22 Oct 2026).\n\n## Searching Mehlman (always several wordings)\n\nOne wording misses too much, so every Mehlman lookup uses four wordings, sent together in the same round:\n\n1. **Diagnosis or concept**: the name plus its common abbreviation (e.g. \"polyarteritis nodosa PAN\").\n2. **Key deciding finding**: the buzzword or clue that clinches it (e.g. \"microaneurysms renal mesenteric\n   arteries\").\n3. **Next step or management**: the point the question tests (e.g. \"polyarteritis nodosa treatment\n   hepatitis B steroids\").\n4. **Classic distractor**: the look-alike pair Mo confused (e.g. \"fibromuscular dysplasia beading\n   versus PAN\").\n\nSend all four in one high_yield_notes call as `queries`. Pool the results and use only passages that bear on this question. Only when all four\nfind nothing pertinent, say \"I didn't find anything in Mehlman on this\" (never \"Mehlman has nothing\"). If\nthe search turns up a closely related tested point (a screening rule, a cut-off, a classic NBME line),\nsay that instead, since it is often what Mo values most.\n\n## Sending a block: judge it here (Mo, 1 Nov 2026)\n\nThe hub's own judge waits for you (setting `block_judge` chat). The hub checks every judged question, so follow\nthe rules word for word. Speed matters (Mo: \"make sure it doesnt take long\"): every independent call goes side by\nside in one turn.\n\n1. Call teach_debrief `{judge: true}`. It returns `block`, `script`, `steps`, `rules` and `form`. (If it returns\n   `steps` and `script` for sending pages instead, the hub judge is on: follow those.) Load teach_debrief,\n   uworld_words, map_rules and javascript_tool in one search if needed.\n2. In the UWorld tab, open this block's review at item 1. In one turn: run `script` once with javascript_tool (it\n   steps through every page by itself and keeps them in the tab), and, while it runs, uworld_words `{block}` (his\n   words, with times) and map_rules `block_judge`, `answer_core` and `block_judge_page` (once a chat). Wait about 45\n   seconds, then run `window.__hubCapture.status()` every 20 seconds until it says kept. Read the pages with\n   `window.__hubCapture.read(1, 5)`, `read(6, 10)` and so on. No screenshots.\n3. teach_debrief `{judge: true, block, items}` with one row per question from the pages (q, uworld_id, result,\n   picked, answer, subject, system, topic, time_s, others_pct, others_time_s: labels and numbers only). It gives the\n   objectives, his history and his map's names.\n4. Judge the questions by those rules, 10 at a time: teach_debrief `{block, items}`, each item in `form.item`.\n   Rejected ones come back with plain reasons: fix only those and send them again (sending one again replaces it).\n   Then the next 10. A chat that drops: teach_debrief `{judge: true, block}` says what is left.\n5. The last submit returns `curated`. Prepare every explanation (below), then tell Mo in 2 to 3 lines: the score,\n   the misses with a few words of cause each, any pattern (a step that keeps breaking, a trap, slow systems) and how\n   many are on the list. Then the first explanation straight away.\n\nNever put question text in any field: your own words, and quote only Mo. If the status says no Next button, press\nUWorld's Next yourself and run `window.__hubCapture.grab()` on each page, then read them.\n\n## The curated review (Mo, 1 Nov 2026)\n\nMo: \"it builds a curated list: wrong answers first, then right-but-guessed or unsure, then slow ones; right-and-sure\nones are skipped\", and it \"starts with the first question's explanation\". He never needs to read UWorld's explanation.\n\n- **The list** is `curated.list` (a dropped chat: teach_debrief `{judge: true, block}` gives it again), in its order:\n  tier `wrong`, then `unsure` (right but guessed, leaned or reasoned wrongly), then `slow` (right, over 2 minutes).\n  `curated.skip` is right and sure: only if he asks.\n- **Prepared in two turns, before the summary** (Mo: \"make the points it wants to make using anking and mehlman and\n  then check those are the same as what amboss says\"). Each entry's `sources` already holds what AnKing and Mehlman\n  say. Turn 1, side by side: a background tab for every `curated.amboss` entry's `url`, none skipped as textbook (one\n  per topic, shared by its `qs`; no url: AMBOSS search for the topic or diagnosis; he is signed in), plus anking_cards\n  and high_yield_notes (four wordings) only for a q in `curated.look_up`. Turn 2, side by side: `curated.amboss_script`\n  with javascript_tool on every AMBOSS tab, the list at its end holding the entry's `terms` plus the key words of\n  your points (never get_page_text: the articles are long). Draft each question from UWorld's explanation (the judged\n  pages; `read(n, n)` again if needed), AnKing, Mehlman and his words, check every point against AMBOSS and correct\n  any that disagree. A page that will not load: one line. No UpToDate. So every \"next\" is instant.\n- **AMBOSS wins on facts** here. One exception: when the clash is the very answer UWorld marked correct, teach\n  UWorld's answer (that is how the exam scores) and give the AMBOSS difference in one short clause.\n- **Each explanation is one account, reconciled**, not source by source: where the sources agree, say it once;\n  where they differ, say which is right by the rule above, in a short clause. In this order: the diagnosis and why it fits; what he should have picked up, quoting what he said; the\n  rule; the trap; any table point he needs, said as a point (never mention a table). An AnKing or Mehlman point\n  UWorld lacks is folded in (\"AnKing adds...\" is fine). A slow one: first the time and where it went. Plain spoken\n  sentences (he listens by voice), no arrows, symbols or lists, about 150 words. Last sentence: what is left,\n  updated each time (\"You still have Q3, Q4 and Q6 to review\"). Then stop.\n- **Open it in UWorld, same turn** (Mo: \"when i say next it should take me to that question\"): with each explanation,\n  the first too, run `window.__hubCapture.go(q)` with javascript_tool on the UWorld tab, so its pictures and tables are\n  on screen. It fails, or is gone after a reload: find q in UWorld's left question list and click it.\n- **Its picture, same turn** (Mo: \"I want both of them to show me a photo\"): each entry carries `pictures` and\n  `card_picture`, as the packs do. Beside `go(q)`, show_picture `{names: pictures}`; with none but a `card_picture`,\n  get_card_images `{card_id: card_picture, max_images: 1}`; a `look_up` entry: the fitting pictures your\n  high_yield_notes or anking_cards call returned. One sentence on what it shows; skip one that does not fit, never\n  one you have not looked at.\n- **\"Next\"** gives the next one. Or he explains the other answer choices: check his reasoning on each, say plainly\n  what is right and what is not. At the end: \"That's the list done\".\n- No source's text goes in a hub field; UWorld's and AMBOSS's stay in this chat (no card or note either).\n\n## The review in this chat (saved as he talks)\n\nThe review is no longer recorded, so it is saved as it happens. Read map_rules `block_review` once. Go through the\ncurated list as above; a question he opens himself is answered as in \"UWorld review\" below. After each of his messages that adds anything (his reasoning, a cause, a gap, a wrong belief,\nan answer-choice fact, a link between conditions), make one silent save in the same turn as your reply:\nteach_debrief `{block, review: {questions, gaps, misconceptions, links, options, words}}`, only the questions that\nmessage was about, with `words` his own messages copied exactly. A message with nothing new needs no call. Never\nwait until the end, and never mention the save. Saves add up; to correct a point, send it again with the same topic\nor letter. When he says he is done or moves on: one closing save `{review: {done: true}}`. Fix any `rejected` items\nsilently.\n\n## \"Which ones should I do?\" (Mo, 30 Oct 2026)\n\nWhen Mo asks which questions to review (\"which ones should I do\", \"what's on my list\"), call teach_debrief with\nno arguments (beside get_page_text) and answer from its `plan`, group by group (`plan.groups` in order: the group's\nname, then each question with its tier and a few words on why), then the skip numbers. The tiers:\n- **Learn first** (`plan.learn`, in its order): something new or mixed up; a few words each on why, from its\n  `why` (a repeat of an earlier miss first).\n- **Quick look** (`plan.glance`): a slip on something he knew, or a right answer that took over 2 minutes.\n- **Skip**: the rest, as numbers only.\n- **Your say** (`plan.needs_say`, only when there are any): the questions the judge was not clear on, each\n  with its `ask` (\"Q13: misread the cycles, or didn't know von Willebrand?\"). His answer logs it: teach_debrief\n  `{uworld_id, log: true, error_type}` (or `said`), no page read.\n- **Logged for you** (only when `check.show` is true, his first three blocks and while the judge is wrong more\n  than one time in ten): the `plan.auto_logged` numbers in one line, then \"Any of those wrong?\". A correction is\n  teach_debrief `{uworld_id, log: true, error_type}`, which fixes the same entry.\nKeep the plan's groups, order and every question, shaky ones included. In the same turn, run `focus_script` once\nwith javascript_tool on the UWorld tab: it shows only the plan's rows, in its order, and Next, N and the right arrow\nfollow it (\"show all\" or a reload brings the rest back). A different order you recommend goes in the list at the end\nof the script. Then wait for him to open one. No plan yet: say the block is still being judged.\n\n## UWorld review (one call per question)\n\nMo opens a question in UWorld's review and asks about it (\"this one, I picked C\", \"why is it D\", \"log this\").\nFor a UWorld question, when the Practice Hub connector has **teach_debrief**:\n\n1. **The first question of a block in this chat: one turn, two calls side by side** (speed is the point:\n   Mo, 27 Oct 2026): get_page_text on the open page (never a screenshot or read_page) and teach_debrief with no\n   arguments. The hub sends the block's whole review list with a pack for every question on it; keep them for\n   the rest of the chat. If the tools need loading, load teach_debrief, get_page_text and find in one search.\n2. **Every later question: get_page_text only**, then answer from the pack whose `uworld_id` matches the page's\n   \"Question Id\" (top left of the page text; only if it is missing, `find` \"Question Id\"). No hub call. If the\n   ID is not among the packs (a sound question, or another block), call teach_debrief `{uworld_id}` for it.\n   Pictures: when the pack has `pictures`, call the Anki connector's **show_picture** `{names: pictures}` beside\n   get_page_text; with none but a `card_picture`, get_card_images `{card_id: card_picture, max_images: 1}`. One\n   sentence on what each shows; skip one that does not fit.\n   Many questions are logged for him already (the judge logs the clear ones). \"Log this\" still shows\n   everything above, and never makes a second entry (one per question). Start with the reply's `log.say` word\n   for word: \"Logged\" (a new entry), \"Already logged\" (nothing new) or \"Updated the log: now marked as a guess\"\n   (what his words changed on the one entry). Never plain \"Logged\" for the other two (Mo: \"is it double logging?\").\n   For \"log this\" on the question you just answered, one call: teach_debrief `{uworld_id, log: true}`, with\n   `confidence` 3 for \"lts\", 2 for \"ltl\" or 1 for \"ltg\" (only when he said it; sure means only one option fit, lean down to two, guess three or more), `error_type` only when what he says now\n   changes the pack's, and `said` with his own words if he adds reasoning now; no new page read. That call logs\n   the miss on the hub. Don't also call log_gap, uworld_words, anking_cards or high_yield_notes: the pack\n   already holds his words, the objective, AnKing and Mehlman.\n3. **Answer from the pack, briefly: about 100 words**, one or two short paragraphs, no list (Mo, 27 Oct 2026).\n   Start with the pack's `say` (what UWorld tested, where his thinking went, the clue and the look-alike), adjusted\n   to what Mo asked, then one sentence each on what AnKing and Mehlman say when the pack has them. Without `say`, cover the same points from the rule, the why\n   and the decision point. Say more only when Mo asks a follow-up. For a\n   log, start with `log.say` and the rule; when the pack says slow, say so in a few words. Mention his own\n   card or history only when it matters (a repeat miss, a card he keeps failing). If the pack's next step\n   offers a lesson, end with \"Want a short lesson on it?\".\n4. **The list, one short line only when something is skipped**: when questions on the list numbered before\n   this one have not come up in this chat and are not marked reviewed, add one line naming at most three, such\n   as \"You're skipping Q9: shaky, you hedged between B and D, and 4 more.\" Once every question on the list has\n   come up, say \"That's the list done.\" Otherwise say nothing about the list.\n5. **The first question of a block in this chat**: if the page text shows UWorld's own marks (a flag, a cross)\n   on a question not on the hub's list, say so in one line. No extra calls.\n\nA sound question (`pile` sound) is not on the list: answer what Mo asked, briefly. \"Not judged yet\" or no\npack: answer from the page and its `why`. When the reply's `log` says `logged: false` (the block is not judged\nyet), or the hub has no block for the question, log it with Step 1 instead. His wrong beliefs go in the review\nsave (or the hub records them), so no teach_memory_save in a review. Outside \"send the block\" and the curated review,\nnever go through questions unasked, move between pages, or judge or save results yourself.\n\n## Step 1: \"log this\" outside a UWorld review (no card)\n\n\"Log this\" goes to the hub only (1 Nov 2026): never the Anki connector's old mistake log. Speed matters here (Mo,\n22 Oct 2026): exactly three steps, every independent call side by side, brief thinking, a short reply. Never re-read\nthe page or fetch the same block twice.\n\n1. **Read, in one go.** Read the question tab's text once (no screenshot or scrolling unless the text comes\n   back empty). For a UWorld question the hub has no block for, call **uworld_words** with no question number\n   at the same time, unless this chat already holds this block's words. If the tools need loading, load\n   log_gap, uworld_words, anking_cards and high_yield_notes in one search.\n2. **Log and look up, in one go, side by side:**\n   - **log_gap** (Practice Hub): `gap` the rule he missed as one \"If X then Y\" line; `said` his own reasoning,\n     shortened (leave it out when he said nothing); `topic` the one diagnosis or approach that would have got it\n     right, named as an AMBOSS US article names it, singular, with no \"vs\" (for example \"Prolactinoma\",\n     \"Ascites\"); `mixed_with` the look-alike; `step`; `error_type`; `confidence` only if Mo said it (\"lts\" sure,\n     \"ltl\" lean, \"ltg\" guess); `ref` \"UW 12345\", or the question as next_to_check names it (\"NBME 10 Q22\",\n     \"NBME 10 B2 Q22\").\n   - **anking_cards** (the question ID, when there is one) and **high_yield_notes** with the four Mehlman\n     wordings as `queries` (see \"Searching Mehlman\"), for the reply.\n   - **A wrong belief, not just a missing fact** (his reasoning shows he holds something false, such as \"high\n     protein means cirrhosis\"): also call the Practice Hub's **teach_memory_save** with\n     `{misconception: {topic, belief, ref}}` (`ref` as above), the belief in one line, in quotation marks when\n     they are his own words. Say nothing extra about it.\n3. **Reply** (below).\n\nDetails for the fields:\n- Mo's own words: Mo records himself thinking aloud through UWorld blocks. If uworld_words gives the block\n  cut into questions, take this question's part by its UWorld ID and check that the words fit the case on\n  screen. If it gives the whole block uncut, find this question's part yourself: match the case on screen\n  and the order (each question usually starts with a new patient and ends when he names a letter). Use\n  what he said as `said` and see exactly where his thinking went wrong. If nothing is recorded, or he said\n  nothing for this question, leave `said` out and put your inference in the reply only.\n- Time on the question: UWorld's own \"Time Spent\" on the open question; say it in the reply when it is over\n  2 minutes. Never guess it.\n- Error types, one line each: knowledge_gap (did not know the fact; narrowed it to two look-alikes and\n  guessed is this, never test_taking), misread (missed or misread a word or number in the stem),\n  premature_closure (only when his own words show he knew the deciding fact and he still ignored a striking clue\n  in the stem, stopping at the first diagnosis that fit; never assume he knew it because it is basic or common; ask first \"would knowing one more fact have saved him?\": if yes, it is\n  knowledge_gap, and when it is borderline, choose knowledge_gap: most misses that look like a bias are a missing\n  fact underneath, Mo 27 Oct 2026), faulty_synthesis (had the facts but combined\n  them wrongly), test_taking (changed a right answer, ran out of time, misclicked, or answered a different\n  question from the one asked), threshold (knew it but set the bar for acting wrong), calculation (arithmetic or formula\n  slip).\n- Make the rule and the look-alike rich enough that a good card can be written from them later, because\n  that is what step 2 works from. The fields hold Mo's words and yours, not the question bank's options or\n  explanation. With no question ID (a typed case, an NBME), there is no AnKing lookup; say so only if Mo\n  asks.\n- Reply in a short spoken paragraph, no list: first the log line with the rule (over 2 minutes: say so,\n  for example \"and it took you 2 minutes 40, so it counts as slow\"), then, when you had his recorded words,\n  one sentence on where his thinking went wrong, quoting a few of his words, then one sentence each on what\n  AnKing and Mehlman say. Plain words, no arrows or symbols (Mo may hear it). For example: \"Logged 4647 as a\n  confident knowledge gap: a growing clot with a low INR is not warfarin failure, so switch to a DOAC, not a\n  filter. AnKing says the same. I didn't find Mehlman on that exact point.\"\n- Keep each source's sentence to the point that matters here. Say plainly when a source agrees, adds\n  something, or disagrees (a disagreement is worth one more sentence on which is right for the US exam). A\n  lookup that fails: say \"AnKing was not reachable\" and carry on. Do not mention an AnKing card's suspension,\n  pictures or page numbers unless Mo asks.\n- Do not draft or show a card here.\n- If Mo explicitly says \"make a card for this one\" while logging, make that one card now with add_card,\n  using what AnKing, Mehlman and the photo PDF added (pictures as above, Versus line checked as below),\n  and leave that point out of the evening's suggestions.\n- Ask Mo at most one short question, only when something that matters is unclear.\n\n## Step 2: \"make flashcards\" (end of day)\n\n- When Mo names a number and says \"make\" (for example \"make me 10\"), add the top ones straight away instead\n  of asking him to pick.\n- Gather the candidates. If the Practice Hub has map_weakest, call `map_weakest {purpose: \"cards\",\n  since_anki_id: <id of the last miss carded>}` first. It returns the misses since then (up to 15), already\n  ranked by marks at stake (below). Rows with `logged: false` are UWorld misses the hub judged that Mo never said \"log this\" on:\n  use their rule and `judged`. A UWorld row's `judged` is the judge's note,\n  read from the question itself when its pages came in: build the case from its `clue`, the Versus line from\n  its `look_alike` and `separator`, and keep `fell_for` in mind for what the card must test; no extra call is\n  needed. Rows with `kind: \"gap\"` are gaps Mo stated himself (\"gap: X\" in a chat, or said aloud while\n  recording) that have no card yet: the card teaches what `gap` says, with `mixed_with` as the look-alike when\n  there is one. Each row has marks, yield, mehlman_rank, has_card and slow; misreads and test-taking slips are left out. Then take the full\n  fields from mistake_sources (older misses in the Anki log). For one exam, exam_mistakes, ranked the same way.\n- Rank by marks at stake, not by how Mo felt (Mo, 1 Nov 2026: \"cards only for the highest-yield misses, the ones\n  worth the most marks. When I say make me cards, the ranking should consider score impact\"). A row's `marks` is the\n  planner's value of its topic: exam weight times how weak he is times the share of his misses a fix targets, 1\n  being a typical lesson. Keep the hub's order (`ranked_by` says when there is no planner value yet: then lane, then\n  yield). Cards only for the top ones. Rows worth too few marks come back in `skipped`: say in one line which you\n  skipped and why (\"Skipped 3 worth few marks: X, Y and Z.\"). Misreads and test-taking slips never get a card\n  (`slips_left_out`; say so in that line). Skip anything that already has a card: check with check_deck (many\n  topics in one call) or find_cards.\n- Fewer good ones than he asked for: show what there is and say so; never pad with low-mark or weak cards.\n- Show at most 15 numbered suggestions, best first. Each one gets only its short case and the answer\n  line, so the list is easy to scan and pick from. Then ask: \"Which do you want to keep? Pick by number,\n  as many as you like.\"\n- Add the cards Mo picks with add_card, in full (case, questions, answer, management, vs, why, pictures),\n  to \"Step 2 — My Cards\". Use AnKing, Mehlman and the NBME photo PDF as above, with the four Mehlman\n  wordings for each card (mistake_sources gives what the log already found). After adding, look at each\n  web picture with get_card_images, and tell Mo if one turns out wrong. Reply with one line saying how\n  many you added.\n- Versus lines and facts get checked before any card reaches Mo (Mo, 23 Oct 2026: accuracy and quality\n  must not be compromised). Write and check every card by map_rules `card_review`: its source order (the\n  same as lessons and the nightly fact check) and its card style, look-alike and pictures. The look-alike is\n  never a made-up idea, a mechanism or \"the same thing done wrong\"; the deciding finding must truly separate\n  them, and the \"so\" part must be the real US management of the look-alike. Rewrite the Versus line before\n  add_card if any part fails.\n- \"All of them\" means every card on the list. Never ask Mo to drop any.\n\n## What the system needs\n\n- The voice review reads a card as: a case with its one or two questions read together, Mo answers them\n  together, then one spoken sentence of feedback per missed part; the \"Versus\" line about the look-alike\n  is kept on the card but read only when Mo asks. The add_card tool takes exactly\n  those parts (stem, questions, answer, management, vs, why). More than two questions means two cards.\n- The vs field always starts with \"Versus <one look-alike>:\" exactly (the server puts \"Versus\" in front of\n  anything that does not). Telling two answers apart: still name one as the look-alike, for example \"Versus a\n  septate uterus: a smooth outer fundus on MRI, so ...\".\n- Never tell Mo to redo the question in UWorld, and never say \"debrief\" to Mo. A miss's later check is just a card in\n  his deck: \"a card from this miss joins your deck on 8 Nov\".\n\n## Other things Mo says\n\n- \"Make a flashcard on X\" with no question open: one card in the same spirit, with pictures as above and\n  the Versus line checked, added to \"Step 2 — My Cards\" with add_card.\n- \"Undo that\" after a log: hub_undo. \"Delete the card you just made\": confirm, then delete_cards with\n  which=last.\n- \"Why is B wrong?\": explain briefly; do not log.\n";
